red: https://t.co/yDoI55b50x
green: https://t.co/VKeEcar1qt

@luka_emon When I started, I didn't understand where the problems lie with AMD. I thought it was the driver, it's not. tinygrad is now submitting AQL queues directly to the GPU.

It's the firmware, and it might even be the hardware.

NVDA deserves to be king.

@karpathy @realGeorgeHotz Thanks!

Yea it's sad watching our ResNet training runs die to a "HW Exception by GPU node-2 (Agent handle: 0x56171a12b050) reason :GPU Hang"

The tenstorrent hardware is fun to think about. We need to move beyond global all-reduce synchronous training.

@study_of_waifu There's two "gimps"

One is P2P support. They disable the P2P mailboxes with e-fuses, but with large BAR support why can't the GPU can't just be a DMA address?

The other is half speed fp32 accumulate instruction dispatch. Fix in algorithm? I hear accumulate in BF16 might work.

Tenstorrent still has a long road to go, and I think aiming for LLM benchmarks and PyTorch integration is dumb. They need to be thinking on a longer timeline than this.

@nvidia has moved the earth to get the interconnect bandwidths to where they are. If you told someone 10 years ago we'd still be doing the "global all-reduce" style of training today...

The future is not global backprop, and Tenstorrent should be thinking about how best to make their HW accessible to people who want to push the paradigm, not hand coding all the stupid current year ML ops.

@tenstorrent @AMD @nvidia True, it's an "all else being equal scenario"

Their Grayskull chip lacks accumulate in float32 and serious memory bandwidth, making it not usable for training. And their user space software is a sprawling mess.

But their driver is solid; "tt-smi" is the nicest smi I have used.
